==============================================================================
6. STATUS OF OP-15 AND WHAT THE FINITE SEARCH DOES NOT SETTLE
==============================================================================
  PROVED HERE (elementary, alphabet-independent):
    halting (finitely many ones) => Thm 4 => max_L p(L) = a_m + 2, a NUMBER.
    Hence NO halting alphabet -- monotone or not -- can have
    sup_L p(L)/L = infinity, nor p(L) = cL for c > 0.
    Monotonicity is NOT needed for this step; it is needed only for the
    converse characterisation 'sum alpha_n >= x <=> halts' (rh41 Thm 9),
    which fails for non-monotone alphabets.

  FAMILY P STRUCTURE (proved here):
    * all-positive periodic pattern -> FINITE support (at most x/min v
      takes) -> bounded constant profile; such patterns are the only
      periodic patterns that can halt/stall.
    * signed periodic pattern -> INFINITE support, never halts (a zero
      residual is not absorbing: a negative term always re-triggers).

  WHAT IS OPEN (this search is finite evidence only):
    whether a NON-halting (infinite-support) non-monotone alphabet can
    realise p(L) growing linearly/superlinearly.  The register's
    conjectural claim 'a halting non-monotone alphabet with
    sup_L p(L)/L = infinity' is NOT settled here; the finite search over
    the stated class found no such object, and (as noted) a halting
    alphabet provably cannot be one, independently of monotonicity.

  DEFINITIONAL CAVEAT (important for reading OP-15).
    * STRONG reading (used above, and in rh41 Thm 9/12): 'halts' = the
      residual reaches exactly 0 in finite time / only finitely many ones.
      Then the impossibility is immediate and monotonicity-free:
      finite support => Thm 4 => max_L p(L) = a_m + 2.
    * WEAK (quasi-halting) reading: 'halts' = r_n -> 0 while infinitely
      many ones are still taken.  This is possible only for non-monotone
      (or non-decreasing) alphabets with alpha_n -> 0: if the residual
      stalls at r > 0 while alpha_n -> 0, then eventually alpha_n < r and
      further ones are forced, so a positive stall cannot occur.  A rare
      example of the non-absorbing phenomenon already appears in the
      periodic family: [1/6,-1/2,1,5/6] at x=1 hits r=0 at n=10 (see the
      'r=0@10' row) yet has infinite support, because a negative term
      re-triggers the process.  Thm 4 does NOT bound the weak regime, and
      it is the genuinely open direction.  The periodic family here cannot
      explore it (positive periodic => finite support; signed periodic =>
      never even quasi-halts), so it remains untouched.

  SEARCH SPACE SIZE:
    values          : 12  (+-j/6, j=1..6)
    patterns k=2,3,4: 12^2 + 12^3 + 12^4 = 22608
    family L cases  : 22608 x 4 = 90432
    family P cases  : 22608 x 2 x N=1024, and
                      top/signed candidates re-run at N up to 4096
    total evaluated : 135648 alphabet/target cases plus growth re-runs

